text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">20 N</text><circle cx="51" cy="214.5" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="51" y1="214.5" x2="95" y2="214.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><path d="M81,214.5 A30,30 0 0,0 76.9,199.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="91.5" y="203.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">30°</text></svg>', 'A 20 N force at 30° above the horizontal (dashed) with its horizontal and vertical components.'),
('48c5f569-cc57-5695-84dd-037f46bec304', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 13, 13, 'What is the size of the upward part of a 20 N force directed 30° above the horizontal?', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '10', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F_y = 20 sin 30° = 10 N. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: sine. A1: F_y = 10 N.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('00f9f269-5661-55f4-8940-b9fb4e9cf7d4', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 14, 14, 'Is temperature a scalar or a vector?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'A scalar', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Temperature has magnitude only. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (A scalar).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('9a84c208-d3c1-5b99-90e0-51763f1c9f6a', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 15, 15, 'Which pair is one scalar and one vector, describing the same motion?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Speed and velocity', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Speed is the magnitude of velocity. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (Speed and velocity).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('8c2c82c0-69d8-5e66-8e9f-5eb0af1d5e46', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 16, 16, 'Two forces of 6.0 N and 8.0 N act at right angles. Calculate the resultant.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '10', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F = √(6.0² + 8.0²) = 10 N. Mark scheme (1 mark). A1: F = 10 N.', 1, NULL, NULL),
('fb8997c7-c577-5932-8b5f-ae39af475668', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 17, 17, 'Why can two scalar quantities of the same kind always be added by ordinary arithmetic?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They have no direction, so only their sizes combine', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'For vectors the direction must be considered. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (They have no direction, so only their sizes combine).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('9ed5bdff-702a-598d-9610-aed90b353dd0', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 18, 18, 'Which of these is not a vector: force, displacement, density, momentum?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Density', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Density is mass per unit volume, a scalar. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (Density).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('3ad33fe5-163e-511a-8e05-5121582373b6', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 19, 19, 'Which of these quantities is a vector: power, work, weight, energy?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Weight', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Weight is a force acting downwards. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (Weight).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('15cba3ea-b245-5448-b3f4-7d56fd440523', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 20, 20, 'Forces of 9.0 N north and 12 N east act on a buoy. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '15', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F = √(9.0² + 12²) = 15 N. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: Pythagoras. A1: F = 15 N.', 2, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 340 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg174713aa-t fg174713aa-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg174713aa-t">Forces on a buoy</title><desc id="fg174713aa-d">Forces of 9.0 N north and 12 N east from a point, with the resultant dashed.</desc><line x1="63.3" y1="210" x2="270.4" y2="210" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="276.7,210 268.5,213.8 268.5,206.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="290.7" y="214" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">12 N</text><line x1="63.3" y1="210" x2="63.3" y2="56.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="63.3,50 67.1,58.2 59.5,58.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="63.3" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">9.0 N</text><line x1="276.7" y1="210" x2="276.7" y2="50" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="63.3" y1="50" x2="276.7" y2="50" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="63.3" y1="210" x2="271.6" y2="53.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="276.7,50 272.4,57.9 267.9,51.9" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="286.7" y="42" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">F</text><circle cx="63.3" cy="210" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="318" y1="50" x2="318" y2="22.9" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="318,18 321,24.3 315,24.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="318" y="9" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">N</text></svg>', 'Forces of 9.0 N north and 12 N east from a point, with the resultant dashed.'),
('46303ea3-22dc-5fe7-adf9-b7804fc0393e', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 21, 21, 'Two forces of 5.0 N and 12 N act on a point at right angles. Calculate the angle between the resultant and the 12 N force.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '22.62', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'θ = tan⁻¹(5.0 ÷ 12) = 22.6°. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: resultant 13 N. C1: tan θ. A1: θ = 22.6°.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 340 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg5888d82d-t fg5888d82d-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg5888d82d-t">Resultant of two forces</title><desc id="fg5888d82d-d">Forces of 12 N and 5.0 N at right angles from a point, with the resultant dashed.</desc><line x1="50.6" y1="179.8" x2="283.1" y2="179.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="289.4,179.8 281.3,183.6 281.3,176" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="303.4" y="183.8" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">12 N</text><line x1="50.6" y1="179.8" x2="50.6" y2="86.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="50.6,80.2 54.4,88.4 46.8,88.4" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="50.6" y="64.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">5.0 N</text><line x1="289.4" y1="179.8" x2="289.4" y2="80.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="50.6" y1="80.2" x2="289.4" y2="80.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="50.6" y1="179.8" x2="283.6" y2="82.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="289.4,80.2 283.4,86.9 280.4,79.9" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="299.4" y="72.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">F</text><circle cx="50.6" cy="179.8" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg>', 'Forces of 12 N and 5.0 N at right angles from a point, with the resultant dashed.'),
('f49fd143-61f6-5524-aa8f-99ca15d7fb1e', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 22, 22, 'A 2.0 kg ball moves at 3.0 m s⁻¹ east. Calculate its momentum and state its direction. Give the magnitude.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6', 'kg m s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'p = 2.0 × 3.0 = 6.0 kg m s⁻¹ east. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: p = mv. C1: direction. A1: p = 6.0 kg m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('7e1407e4-e25f-5b50-8ee9-d9fb2bb9a5c0', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 23, 23, 'A 0.50 kg ball moving at 4.0 m s⁻¹ east rebounds at 4.0 m s⁻¹ west. Calculate the magnitude of its change in momentum.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4', 'kg m s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Δp = 0.50 × (−4.0 − 4.0) = −4.0 kg m s⁻¹, so the magnitude is 0.50 × 8.0 = 4.0 kg m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: vector change. C1: value. A1: Δp = 4.0 kg m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('3124fd00-a4dc-5230-b320-aeb48d1b2757', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 24, 24, 'A ball rebounds with the same speed. Which quantity changes?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Its momentum (but not its kinetic energy)', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Momentum is a vector; kinetic energy is a scalar. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: momentum reverses. C1: KE unchanged. A1: B (Its momentum (but not its kinetic energy)).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('2b0be21f-8780-52fc-9355-60d2442c321c', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 25, 25, 'A box is pulled by a rope with a tension of 80 N at 25° above the horizontal. Calculate the horizontal pull on the box.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '72.5', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F_x = 80 cos 25° = 72.5 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: cosine. C1: value. A1: F_x = 72.5 N.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg091115aa-t fg091115aa-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg091115aa-t">Rope tension</title><desc id="fg091115aa-d">A tension of 80 N at 25° above the horizontal (dashed) with its horizontal and vertical components.</desc><line x1="51" y1="200.2" x2="302.7" y2="200.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="309,200.2 300.9,204 300.9,196.4" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="180" y="214.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)"></text><line x1="309" y1="200.2" x2="309" y2="86.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="309,79.8 312.8,88 305.2,88" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="323" y="144" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)"></text><line x1="51" y1="200.2" x2="303.3" y2="82.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="309,79.8 303.3,86.7 300,79.8" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="185.9" y="152.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">80 N</text><circle cx="51" cy="200.2" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="51" y1="200.2" x2="95" y2="200.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><path d="M81,200.2 A30,30 0 0,0 78.1,187.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="92" y="191.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">25°</text></svg>', 'A tension of 80 N at 25° above the horizontal (dashed) with its horizontal and vertical components.'),
('77529d33-7e0b-55aa-95bc-4a0270da16d5', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 26, 26, 'A force of 50 N acts at 60° to a second force of 50 N. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '86.6', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F = √(50² + 50² + 2 × 50 × 50 × cos 60°) = 86.6 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: parallelogram rule. C1: value. A1: F = 86.6 N.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 340 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg254aa3e0-t fg254aa3e0-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg254aa3e0-t">Two equal forces</title><desc id="fg254aa3e0-d">Two 50 N forces 60° apart from a point, with the resultant dashed by the parallelogram rule.</desc><line x1="50" y1="199.3" x2="203.7" y2="199.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="210,199.3 201.8,203.1 201.8,195.5" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="224" y="203.3" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">50 N</text><line x1="50" y1="199.3" x2="126.9" y2="66.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="130,60.7 129.2,69.7 122.6,65.9" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="137" y="52.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">50 N</text><line x1="210" y1="199.3" x2="290" y2="60.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="130" y1="60.7" x2="290" y2="60.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="50" y1="199.3" x2="284.5" y2="63.9" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="290,60.7 284.8,68.1 281,61.5" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="300" y="52.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">F</text><circle cx="50" cy="199.3" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg>', 'Two 50 N forces 60° apart from a point, with the resultant dashed by the parallelogram rule.'),
('75a38658-d247-5557-bbff-b3785e9fe900', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 27, 27, 'Which statement about pressure is correct?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Pressure is a scalar, even though the force producing it is a vector', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Pressure at a point acts equally in all directions. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: scalar. C1: reason. A1: C (Pressure is a scalar, even though the force producing it is a vector).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('845710a6-c167-5041-8c20-25dd344398ef', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 28, 28, 'An object of weight 50 N rests on a slope at 30° to the horizontal. Calculate the component of its weight down the slope.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '25', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F = 50 sin 30° = 25 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: sine of slope angle. C1: value. A1: F = 25 N.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ffb3c6a2-09de-5633-ad9c-f52971c6bd64', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 29, 29, 'For a 50 N block resting on a 30° incline, how large is the weight component that presses it into the surface?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '43.3', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F = 50 cos 30° = 43.3 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: cosine. C1: value. A1: F = 43.3 N.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('227a5647-d2de-5659-9ae8-b8eefbb0f7dd', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 30, 30, 'Three forces act on a point: 10 N north, 10 N east and 4.0 N south. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '11.66', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'North: 10 − 4.0 = 6.0 N; east: 10 N. F = √(10² + 6.0²) = 11.7 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: net components. C1: Pythagoras. A1: F = 11.7 N.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg930b7b63-t fg930b7b63-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg930b7b63-t">Three forces</title><desc id="fg930b7b63-d">Forces of 10 N north, 10 N east and 4.0 N south drawn head to tail; the resultant is dashed.</desc><line x1="86" y1="234" x2="86" y2="52.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="86,46 89.8,54.2 82.2,54.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="72" y="144" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">10 N</text><line x1="86" y1="46" x2="267.7" y2="46" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="274,46 265.8,49.8 265.8,42.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="180" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">10 N</text><line x1="274" y1="46" x2="274" y2="114.9" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="274,121.2 270.2,113 277.8,113" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="288" y="87.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4.0 N</text><line x1="86" y1="234" x2="268.6" y2="124.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="274,121.2 269,128.7 265,122.1" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="172.8" y="165.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">F</text><circle cx="86" cy="234" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="338" y1="50" x2="338" y2="22.9" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="338,18 341,24.3 335,24.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="338" y="9" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">N</text></svg>', 'Forces of 10 N north, 10 N east and 4.0 N south drawn head to tail; the resultant is dashed.'),
('2dd19ad4-dee4-5f37-b6eb-64352910beb4', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 31, 31, 'Why is the work done by a force a scalar even though force and displacement are vectors?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Work is a product (F s cos θ) that has magnitude only', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Work is energy transferred. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: product of vectors. C1: energy is scalar. A1: A (Work is a product (F s cos θ) that has magnitude only).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('cf94b673-d238-549e-9ce9-a28890d4f316', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 32, 32, 'A 1200 kg car moves at 15 m s⁻¹ north and then 15 m s⁻¹ east. Calculate the magnitude of its change in momentum.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '25456', 'kg m s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '|Δv| = √(15² + 15²) = 21.21 m s⁻¹; |Δp| = 1200 × 21.21 = 2.55 × 10⁴ kg m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (4 marks). M1: vector change. C1: |Δv|. C1: × mass. A1: Δp = 2.55 × 10⁴ kg m s⁻¹.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('d2cf26fa-2272-5e35-9718-21cedab0b7e9', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 33, 33, 'Two forces, 7.0 N and 4.0 N, act on an object. Calculate (largest possible resultant) − (smallest possible resultant).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '8', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Largest = 11 N, smallest = 3.0 N; difference = 11 − 3.0 = 8.0 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: both extremes. C1: difference. A1: ΔF = 8.0 N.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('14a6d0ab-bbf5-5bf3-92fd-2f9206183634', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 34, 34, 'Which list is correctly split into scalars | vectors?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'mass, time, energy | force, velocity, acceleration', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Check each for a direction. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: scalars correct. C1: vectors correct. A1: D (mass, time, energy | force, velocity, acceleration).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('fc86de76-7667-5125-a74d-0b02b89b0e62', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 35, 35, 'A sledge is pulled by two ropes, each with tension 150 N, at 20° either side of the direction of motion. Calculate the resultant forward force.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '281.9', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Sideways components cancel. F = 2 × 150 × cos 20° = 282 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: symmetry. C1: forward components. A1: F = 282 N.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 340 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg13b69688-t fg13b69688-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg13b69688-t">Two tow ropes</title><desc id="fg13b69688-d">Two 150 N tensions at 20° either side of the direction of motion, with the resultant dashed.</desc><line x1="53.7" y1="130" x2="164.1" y2="89.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="170,87.7 163.6,94 161,86.9" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="183.2" y="86.9" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">150 N</text><line x1="53.7" y1="130" x2="164.1" y2="170.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="170,172.3 161,173.1 163.6,166" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="183.2" y="181.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">150 N</text><line x1="170" y1="87.7" x2="286.3" y2="130" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="170" y1="172.3" x2="286.3" y2="130" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="53.7" y1="130" x2="280" y2="130" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="286.3,130 278.2,133.8 278.2,126.2" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="296.3" y="122" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">F</text><circle cx="53.7" cy="130" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg>', 'Two 150 N tensions at 20° either side of the direction of motion, with the resultant dashed.'),
('0a73e8e8-02ab-5e02-bb1e-4ed8806f2754', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 36, 36, 'A rope pulls with 40 N at 35° to the horizontal. How much of this pull acts vertically upwards?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '22.94', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F_y = 40 sin 35° = 22.9 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: sine. C1: value. A1: F_y = 22.9 N.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('97ecd144-f505-5643-9d18-c6cc6fbefc57', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 37, 37, 'Two forces of equal size act on a point and the resultant is zero. What must be true?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They act in exactly opposite directions', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Equal and opposite vectors cancel. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: opposite. C1: equal size. A1: B (They act in exactly opposite directions).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('c7b08ae9-47c1-50a7-9200-ec78e1237985', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 38, 38, 'The resultant of two perpendicular forces is 26 N. One force is 10 N. Calculate the other.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '24', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F = √(26² − 10²) = 24 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: Pythagoras rearranged. C1: value. A1: F = 24 N.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('9a8df45f-36ab-5a73-8c09-302170ebce7d', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 39, 39, 'A 3.0 kg mass hangs at rest. Calculate its weight and state its direction. Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '29.43', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'W = mg = 3.0 × 9.81 = 29.4 N, vertically downwards. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: W = mg. C1: direction. A1: W = 29.4 N.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('03e733c8-a5c5-5d35-b571-c2e9e1b24569', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 40, 40, 'A car''s speedometer shows 20 m s⁻¹ and its compass shows due north. Which quantity do the two readings together give?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Its velocity', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Velocity = speed with a direction. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: magnitude. C1: direction. A1: D (Its velocity).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ac7cea80-e88f-5118-b757-351333c8390d', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 41, 41, 'A kite string pulls with 25 N at 50° to the horizontal. Calculate the horizontal component of the pull.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '16.07', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F_x = 25 cos 50° = 16.1 N. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: cosine. C1: value. A1: F_x = 16.1 N.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg38b89a3c-t fg38b89a3c-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg38b89a3c-t">Kite string</title><desc id="fg38b89a3c-d">A 25 N pull at 50° to the horizontal (dashed) with its horizontal and vertical components.</desc><line x1="101.1" y1="234" x2="252.6" y2="234" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="258.9,234 250.7,237.8 250.7,230.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="180" y="248" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)"></text><line x1="258.9" y1="234" x2="258.9" y2="52.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="258.9,46 262.7,54.2 255.1,54.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="272.9" y="144" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)"></text><line x1="101.1" y1="234" x2="254.8" y2="50.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="258.9,46 256.5,54.7 250.7,49.8" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="190.7" y="149" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">25 N</text><circle cx="101.1" cy="234" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="101.1" y1="234" x2="145.1" y2="234" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><path d="M131.1,234 A30,30 0 0,0 120.4,211" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="139.2" y="216.3" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">50°</text></svg>', 'A 25 N pull at 50° to the horizontal (dashed) with its horizontal and vertical components.'),
('b75198b6-dcc6-5c0c-9a53-8a02cc4a2dc6', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 42, 42, 'Three forces act on a ring: 20 N east, 30 N at 60° north of east and 25 N south. (a) Find the east and north components of the resultant. (b) Calculate its magnitude.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '35.01', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) East: 20 + 30 cos 60° = 35 N; north: 30 sin 60° − 25 = 0.981 N. (b) F = √(35² + 0.981²) = 35.0 N. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: east. C1: north. M1: Pythagoras. C1: value. A1: F = 35.0 N.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgec31c9b9-t fgec31c9b9-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgec31c9b9-t">Three forces on a ring</title><desc id="fgec31c9b9-d">Forces of 20 N east, 30 N at 60° north of east and 25 N south drawn head to tail; the resultant is dashed.</desc><line x1="53.4" y1="234" x2="191.8" y2="234" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="198.1,234 189.9,237.8 189.9,230.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="125.7" y="248" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">20 N</text><line x1="198.1" y1="234" x2="303.5" y2="51.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="306.6,46 305.8,55 299.3,51.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="264.5" y="147" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">30 N</text><line x1="306.6" y1="46" x2="306.6" y2="220.6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="306.6,226.9 302.8,218.7 310.4,218.7" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="320.6" y="140.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">25 N</text><line x1="53.4" y1="234" x2="300.3" y2="227.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="306.6,226.9 298.6,230.9 298.4,223.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="179.6" y="216.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">F</text><circle cx="53.4" cy="234" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg>', 'Forces of 20 N east, 30 N at 60° north of east and 25 N south drawn head to tail; the resultant is dashed.'),
('e0130d2a-8b31-50f0-aebd-198e21c150ba', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 43, 43, 'A 0.15 kg ball hits a wall at 20 m s⁻¹ at 45° to the wall and rebounds at 20 m s⁻¹ at 45° on the other side. (a) Explain which component of velocity changes. (b) Calculate the magnitude of the change in momentum. (c) State its direction.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.243', 'kg m s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Only the component perpendicular to the wall reverses. (b) |Δp| = 0.15 × 2 × 20 × sin 45° = 4.24 kg m s⁻¹. (c) Perpendicular to the wall, away from it. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: perpendicular component reverses. C1: component. M1: doubled × mass. C1: value. B1: direction. A1: Δp = 4.24 kg m s⁻¹.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('3c506b3c-f878-5e6e-baf2-7ccde369cdad', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 44, 44, 'Two forces of 8.0 N and 6.0 N act at 60° to each other. (a) Calculate the magnitude of the resultant. (b) Calculate the angle between the resultant and the 8.0 N force.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '25.28', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Components along 8.0 N: 8.0 + 6.0 cos 60° = 11 N; across: 6.0 sin 60° = 5.196 N; F = √(11² + 5.196²) = 12.2 N. (b) θ = tan⁻¹(5.196 ÷ 11) = 25.3°. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: components. C1: magnitude. M1: tan θ. C1: value. A1: θ = 25.3°.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 340 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg156c3ec7-t fg156c3ec7-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg156c3ec7-t">Forces at 60°</title><desc id="fg156c3ec7-d">Forces of 8.0 N and 6.0 N acting at 60° to each other, with the resultant dashed.</desc><line x1="50.6" y1="186.4" x2="218" y2="186.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="224.3,186.4 216.1,190.2 216.1,182.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="238.3" y="190.4" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">8.0 N</text><line x1="50.6" y1="186.4" x2="112.6" y2="79" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="115.7,73.6 114.9,82.5 108.3,78.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="122.7" y="65.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">6.0 N</text><line x1="224.3" y1="186.4" x2="289.4" y2="73.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="115.7" y1="73.6" x2="289.4" y2="73.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="50.6" y1="186.4" x2="283.7" y2="76.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="289.4,73.6 283.7,80.5 280.4,73.6" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="299.4" y="65.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">F</text><circle cx="50.6" cy="186.4" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg>', 'Forces of 8.0 N and 6.0 N acting at 60° to each other, with the resultant dashed.'),
('5c57fac2-3a46-54b8-932d-8c5d571514f1', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 45, 45, 'A 2.0 kg trolley moves at 3.0 m s⁻¹ east and a 1.5 kg trolley at 4.0 m s⁻¹ north. (a) Calculate each momentum. (b) Calculate the magnitude of the total momentum. (c) Calculate the total kinetic energy, and explain why it is found by simple addition.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '21', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 6.0 kg m s⁻¹ east; 6.0 kg m s⁻¹ north. (b) p = √(6.0² + 6.0²) = 8.49 kg m s⁻¹. (c) E = 0.5 × 2.0 × 3.0² + 0.5 × 1.5 × 4.0² = 21 J; energy is a scalar. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: momenta. M1: vector addition. C1: magnitude. C1: KE. B1: scalar. A1: E = 21 J.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('96c8ad59-e6b5-53ca-bfb9-5ad7964ceb4e', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 46, 46, 'A lamp of weight 40 N hangs from two cables, each at 30° to the vertical. (a) Explain why the horizontal components of tension cancel. (b) Calculate the tension in each cable.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '23.09', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The cables are symmetrical and the lamp is in equilibrium. (b) 2T cos 30° = 40, so T = 40 ÷ (2 × cos 30°) = 23.1 N. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: symmetry/equilibrium. M1: vertical components = weight. C1: rearrange. C1: value. A1: T = 23.1 N.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('94b54ecc-3165-5aa5-95a4-7e50f398dc18', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 47, 47, 'Forces of 15 N north, 20 N east and F act on a point in equilibrium. (a) Explain what equilibrium means for the vector sum. (b) Calculate the magnitude of F. (c) Calculate its direction as an angle west of south.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '53.13', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The resultant of all three is zero. (b) F = √(15² + 20²) = 25 N. (c) F points south-west: θ = tan⁻¹(20 ÷ 15) = 53.1° west of south. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: zero resultant. M1: F equal and opposite to resultant of others. C1: magnitude. M1: angle. C1: value. A1: θ = 53.1°.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('8636eeb8-28c4-5314-82d7-79d250d48dbd', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 48, 48, 'A crate is pulled with 120 N at 30° above the horizontal while friction is 70 N. (a) Calculate the horizontal component of the pull. (b) Calculate the resultant horizontal force.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '33.92', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 120 cos 30° = 103.9 N. (b) F = 103.9 − 70 = 33.9 N. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: component. C1: value. M1: subtract friction. C1: value. A1: F = 33.9 N.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 360 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg3fc86394-t fg3fc86394-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg3fc86394-t">Pulling a crate</title><desc id="fg3fc86394-d">A pull of 120 N at 30° above the horizontal (dashed) with its horizontal and vertical components.</desc><line x1="57.7" y1="210.6" x2="296" y2="210.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="302.3,210.6 294.2,214.4 294.2,206.8" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="180" y="224.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)"></text><line x1="302.3" y1="210.6" x2="302.3" y2="75.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="302.3,69.4 306.1,77.5 298.5,77.5" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="316.3" y="144" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)"></text><line x1="57.7" y1="210.6" x2="296.9" y2="72.5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><polygon points="302.3,69.4 297.2,76.8 293.4,70.2" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="187" y="152.1" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">120 N</text><circle cx="57.7" cy="210.6" r="3.4" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="57.7" y1="210.6" x2="101.7" y2="210.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><path d="M87.7,210.6 A30,30 0 0,0 83.7,195.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="98.2" y="199.8" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">30°</text></svg>', 'A pull of 120 N at 30° above the horizontal (dashed) with its horizontal and vertical components.'),
('631db879-1feb-5bb0-b498-c3d0df51702a', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 49, 49, 'A boat is towed by two tugs. Tug A pulls with 4.0 kN at 20° to the boat''s axis and tug B with 5.0 kN at 15° on the other side. (a) Calculate the forward component of each pull. (b) Calculate the total forward force, in kN. (c) Calculate the net sideways force, in kN.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '-0.07399 kN', 'kN', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 4.0 cos 20° = 3.759 kN; 5.0 cos 15° = 4.830 kN. (b) 8.59 kN. (c) 5.0 sin 15° − 4.0 sin 20° = −0.074 kN towards the side of tug B. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: forward A. C1: forward B. C1: total. M1: sideways components. C1: value. A1: F_side = −0.074 kN.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('90485eb8-143b-57c3-bf6f-85929beffda1', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 50, 50, 'An aircraft of mass 2.0 × 10⁵ kg changes velocity from 220 m s⁻¹ east to 220 m s⁻¹ north. (a) Calculate |Δv|. (b) Calculate the magnitude of the change in momentum, in units of 10⁷ kg m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6.223', '10⁷ kg m s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) |Δv| = √(220² + 220²) = 311.1 m s⁻¹. (b) |Δp| = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 311.1 = 6.22 × 10⁷ kg m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (5 marks). M1: perpendicular velocities. C1: |Δv|. M1: × mass. C1: value. A1: Δp = 6.22 × 10⁷ kg m s⁻¹.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('da6efa9c-a871-50a9-b25e-f545fddc7f8f', '4653a171-76f9-4b44-bc1d-19802787195f', 'e1b6c84c-afbc-4845-b67b-d5b6bd15720c', 'a-level', '1.8', '9702 coursebook 1.8 · Other examples of scalar and vector quantities', 51, 51, 'Four forces act on a point: 10 N north, 10 N east, 10 N south and an unknown force F west. The resultant is 4.0 N east. (a) Explain why the north and south forces cancel. (b) Calculate F.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6', 'N', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) They are equal and opposite. (b) 10 − F = 4.0, so F = 10 − 4.0 = 6.0 N. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: cancel. M1: east − west = resultant. C1: rearrange. C1: value. A1: F = 6.0 N.', 5, NULL, NULL)
